    "q-143552": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143552",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\mu \\cdot \\sigma -\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16})$\n\nII. $\\mu -\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{16})$\n\nIII. $\\sigma -max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{15})$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expressions I and II are *not* statistics. They are functions that contain the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
