    "q-143549": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143549",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is known and $\\sigma $ is unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${X}_{1}+{X}_{20}-\\mu $\n\nII. ${X}_{2}+{X}_{19}-\\sigma $\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\mu }{{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
