    "q-149834": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149834",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n17.4,\\,18.1,\\,19.2,\\,17.8,\\,18.7\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{max}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})}{n+1}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n- $\\text{max}({x}_{1},…,{x}_{5})=19.2,$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $\\frac{19.2}{6}=3.2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
